# Hooks and guards

- A hook runs on every tool call. No new top-level import in `hookmain.py`, `hookboot.py`, `guards.py`; import inside
  the function or use `lazy.LazyModule`.
- Time tests use CPU time or an injected clock, never wall-clock.
- Strictness is the contract: a guard may become more permissive only for a documented false positive, with a test that
  the true positive is still denied.
- New guard case: on a clean tree `python3 guard_corpus.py --check` must print `0 differences`; append to the END of
  `BASH_MULTI` (or add to `FILE_PATHS`/`MCP`/`OTHER_TOOLS`); `--write`; read `git diff --stat tests/data/guard_decisions.json`
  (only added rows). `--write` is never a way to fix a failing replay.
- Run `test_hook_latency`, `test_guards`, `test_guards2`.
- Loops need a cap and a test of the cap; waits need a deadline; "stuck" is a pure predicate (see `fastwatch.py`).
